::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which focus is strongest for an informative speech?
{
~A. Presentations are things people do.#This is too vague to guide a speech.
=B. Organized preparation helps speakers deliver clearer presentations.#Correct. This focus is specific and supports organized informative speaking.
~C. I might talk about school.#This does not give a clear informative purpose.
~D. Many topics could be discussed.#This is broad and unfocused.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which set best develops an informative point?
{
~A. Point only.#This is incomplete because it lacks support and reasoning.
~B. Topic only.#A topic alone is not developed.
~C. Joke, random fact, topic change.#This does not develop information.
=D. Point, support, and reasoning.#Correct. This pattern explains information clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Rehearsing aloud helps speakers notice unclear parts before presenting. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
=A. Practicing out loud can help speakers find confusing sections before the presentation.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning in new words.
~B. Speakers should not revise unclear parts.#This contradicts the source.
~C. Rehearsing aloud makes every speech perfect.#This exaggerates the source meaning.
~D. Presenting means reading silently.#This adds an unrelated and inaccurate idea.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech product follows quality format expectations?
{
~A. A copied source paragraph only.#This is not a speech-ready format.
~B. A title with no main points.#This is incomplete because the speaker needs main points to organize the message.
=C. Introduction, focus, main points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. These parts create a complete speech format.
~D. Random notes with no order.#This does not support presentation quality.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which structure best explains a process?
{
~A. Unrelated examples.#Unrelated examples do not explain a process.
~B. Random order.#Random order makes the process hard to follow.
=C. Chronological order.#Correct. A process is clearest when steps are presented in order.
~D. Hidden structure.#Hidden structure does not guide the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence follows standard English conventions?
{
=A. My speech explains how note cards support delivery.#Correct. This sentence uses standard capitalization, grammar, and wording.
~B. my speech explain note card ideas.#This has capitalization and agreement errors, so it is not ready for speech notes.
~C. My speech explain note cards.#This has a subject-verb agreement error.
~D. my speech explains note cards.#This needs capitalization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which phrase uses precise academic speech vocabulary?
{
~A. speaking stuff#This is vague and informal.
~B. talk things#This is not precise academic vocabulary.
~C. whatever words#This is informal and unclear.
=D. coherent focus#Correct. This is precise vocabulary for speech organization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which main point supports the focus "Planning helps speakers communicate clearly"?
{
~A. Some classrooms have windows.#This is unrelated to planning and clear communication.
=B. Outlining ideas helps the audience follow the speech order.#Correct. This point supports planning and clarity.
~C. Many people like music.#This is unrelated because it does not connect to planning or clear speech communication.
~D. Lunch can be scheduled differently.#This does not support the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support best fits "Visual aids clarify information"?
{
=A. A chart can show a pattern that may be hard to explain with words alone.#Correct. This support explains how a visual aid clarifies.
~B. Visual aids are things.#This is too vague because it names the tool but does not explain how it helps the audience understand.
~C. Some posters are colorful.#This does not explain clarity.
~D. Speeches can happen with slides.#This is related but not enough support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which choice is closest to copying rather than paraphrasing?
{
~A. A new sentence that keeps the source meaning.#This describes a paraphrase.
~B. A short note in the speaker's own words.#This can be useful if accurate.
=C. The source sentence with only one word changed.#Correct. This is too close to copying.
~D. A restatement with a new sentence pattern.#This supports paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What makes notes speech-ready?
{
~A. They force the speaker to read every word.#This weakens natural delivery.
=B. They give short cues for focus, points, support, and transitions.#Correct. Short cues support clear delivery.
~C. They include unrelated phrases.#Unrelated phrases do not guide speaking.
~D. They hide the main points.#Speech notes should make points easy to find.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
The word "however" usually signals which relationship?
{
~A. Sequence.#Sequence is signaled by words such as first or next.
~B. Cause.#Cause is signaled by words such as because.
~C. Definition.#However does not define a term.
=D. Contrast.#Correct. However signals a different or opposing idea.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which sentence gives reasoning?
{
~A. Timing is a word.#This does not explain anything about why timing helps the speaker or audience.
~B. A timer can measure a speech.#This is support, not reasoning, because it gives evidence without explaining why it matters.
~C. The next point is notes.#This is a transition because it moves to another topic instead of explaining support.
=D. This matters because timing helps the audience follow without feeling rushed.#Correct. This explains why support matters.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition best connects organization to support?
{
~A. Next thing.#This is vague because it does not show how the next idea connects to the previous idea.
=B. After organizing the points, the speaker should add support that explains each idea.#Correct. This transition shows a clear relationship.
~C. I am changing topics.#This does not connect ideas.
~D. Anyway, support.#This is too casual and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which revision corrects "my notes helps me present"?
{
~A. my notes helps me present#This keeps the capitalization and agreement errors from the original sentence.
~B. My notes helps me present.#This fixes capitalization but not agreement.
=C. My notes help me present.#Correct. This fixes capitalization and subject-verb agreement.
~D. my notes help present ideas.#This still needs capitalization even though the agreement problem has been corrected.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which plan uses cause and effect?
{
=A. Rehearsal builds familiarity, so nervousness may decrease.#Correct. This shows a cause and result.
~B. Silent practice differs from peer practice.#This compares two options.
~C. Speech notes include focus and support.#This lists parts of a plan, but it does not show a cause and an effect.
~D. Choose topic, outline, rehearse.#This is chronological order.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which format part closes the speech?
{
~A. Hidden topic.#A hidden topic weakens the speech.
~B. Random detail.#A random detail does not close the speech.
=C. Conclusion.#Correct. The conclusion closes the speech and returns to the focus.
~D. First support point.#A support point develops the body, not the closing.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support is too general?
{
~A. A timer can show whether the introduction takes too long.#This is specific because it gives a concrete rehearsal tool and purpose.
~B. A transition can show contrast between two ideas.#This is specific because it identifies a communication move and its function.
~C. A note card can list three main points.#This is specific because it names a tool and a clear planning detail.
=D. Practice is good.#Correct. This is too general and does not explain how practice helps.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Why should paraphrased source information keep the original meaning?
{
=A. So the speech communicates borrowed ideas accurately.#Correct. Accurate paraphrase protects the source meaning.
~B. So the speaker can avoid understanding the source.#Understanding is required.
~C. So the speaker can change facts freely.#Changing facts is inaccurate.
~D. So the speaker can remove the main idea.#Removing the main idea weakens accuracy.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which conclusion best fits a speech about preparation?
{
~A. I also like sports.#This is unrelated because it introduces a new topic instead of closing the preparation speech.
=B. Careful preparation helps speakers organize, support, and deliver clearer presentations.#Correct. This conclusion restates the focus and major points.
~C. I am finished.#This does not restate the focus.
~D. There are many things.#This is vague because it does not restate the focus or summarize the speech's key ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which structure compares two practice methods?
{
~A. Chronological order.#This shows time order, but it does not compare two practice methods.
=B. Compare and contrast.#Correct. This structure explains similarities and differences.
~C. Cause and effect.#This explains why a result happens.
~D. Definition.#This explains a term, but it does not show similarities and differences.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word is most precise for a speech plan?
{
=A. transition#Correct. Transition is precise academic vocabulary for speech organization.
~B. stuff#This is vague because it does not identify a clear speech-planning concept.
~C. whatever#This is informal and does not use precise communication vocabulary for a speech plan.
~D. things#This is too general because it does not name a specific speech part or communication action.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which answer best explains the role of support?
{
~A. Support replaces the focus.#The focus is still needed.
~B. Support makes the speech random.#Support should connect to the point.
~C. Support removes reasoning.#Reasoning is still needed.
=D. Support gives details that help explain a point.#Correct. Support helps the audience understand the point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which product is incomplete?
{
~A. Outline with focus, points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#This includes major parts.
~B. Notes with point and support cues.#These can support delivery.
=C. Title only with no speech sections.#Correct. A title alone is incomplete.
~D. Checklist with format and convention checks.#This supports quality work.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which question checks coherent focus?
{
=A. Does every main point support the same central idea?#Correct. This checks whether the speech stays focused.
~B. Is the speech as long as possible?#Length does not prove focus.
~C. Are the points unrelated?#Unrelated points weaken focus.
~D. Did the speaker copy a source?#Copying does not check focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q26::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition signals sequence?
{
~A. However, notes have limits.#However signals contrast, so it does not show the order of steps in a sequence.
~B. In contrast, peer practice differs.#In contrast signals comparison, not sequence.
~C. Because rehearsal helps.#Because signals cause, so it does not show step-by-step order.
=D. First, choose the main point you need to support.#Correct. First signals sequence.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q27::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Note cards can help a speaker remember the order of ideas. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Note cards make ideas unnecessary.#This changes the source meaning.
=B. Note cards can help speakers keep their ideas in order.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning.
~C. Speakers should avoid all notes.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Order does not matter in speeches.#This contradicts the source.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q28::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence is ready for speech notes?
{
~A. the outline show three planning points#This has capitalization and agreement errors, so it is not ready for speech notes.
~B. The outline show three points.#This has an agreement error.
=C. The outline shows three points.#Correct. This sentence follows standard English conventions.
~D. the outline shows the three points.#This needs capitalization before it is ready for speech notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q29::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which checklist item supports speech format?
{
~A. The plan includes only random facts.#Random facts weaken format.
~B. The plan hides the focus.#The focus should be clear because it guides the speaker and audience.
=C. The plan includes transitions between main points.#Correct. Transitions are part of quality speech format.
~D. The plan has no conclusion.#A conclusion is needed because it closes the speech and reinforces the message.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q30::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
The word "therefore" signals what relationship?
{
~A. Contrast.#Contrast is signaled by however.
=B. Result.#Correct. Therefore signals a result or conclusion.
~C. Definition.#Therefore does not define a word.
~D. Random order.#Therefore does not signal random order.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q31::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support should be removed from a speech about note cards?
{
=A. Cafeteria menus change each week.#Correct. This is unrelated to note cards.
~B. Short notes can keep the speaker from reading a full script.#This supports the topic.
~C. A note card can list the three main points.#This supports the topic.
~D. Note cards can include transition cues.#This supports the topic.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q32::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech plan would be hardest for an audience to follow?
{
~A. A plan with chronological order.#This can help an audience follow because ideas are presented in time order.
~B. A plan with clear transitions.#This helps listeners understand how one idea connects to the next.
~C. A plan with related main points.#This supports focus because the ideas connect to the same message.
=D. A plan with random order and unrelated points.#Correct. Random order and unrelated points weaken understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q33::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which phrase best introduces reasoning?
{
~A. Forget that...#This does not explain support.
~B. Randomly...#This does not signal reasoning.
~C. On another topic...#This signals a topic shift.
=D. This matters because...#Correct. This phrase signals explanation of why support matters.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q34::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which statement about paraphrase is true?
{
~A. It should change the source meaning.#This makes the paraphrase inaccurate.
~B. It should copy every word.#Copying is not paraphrasing.
=C. It should restate the source accurately in new wording.#Correct. A paraphrase keeps meaning while changing wording.
~D. It should remove the important idea.#Removing the idea weakens accuracy.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q35::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which note is too vague?
{
=A. Talk about everything.#Correct. This does not give a clear point or support.
~B. Support: timer checks length.#This gives a specific cue.
~C. Point 1: outline order.#This gives a clear cue because it names a point the speaker can follow.
~D. Transition: after notes, rehearse.#This gives a relationship cue.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q36::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which feature signals an example?
{
~A. However#However signals contrast.
=B. For example#Correct. This phrase signals that an example is coming.
~C. Finally#Finally signals sequence or closing.
~D. Because#Because signals cause, so it does not introduce an example.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q37::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which phrase is most academically precise?
{
~A. speaking stuff#This is vague and informal.
=B. speech structure#Correct. This phrase precisely names a communication concept.
~C. talk things#This is too general because it does not name a specific communication concept.
~D. whatever part#This is informal and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q38::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which choice best supports oral presentation?
{
~A. Notes that hide the main point.#This weakens delivery because the speaker may lose the central idea.
~B. Random source sentences.#Random sentences do not guide delivery.
~C. A copied paragraph with no labels.#This may cause reading and confusion.
=D. Notes with short cues for each main point.#Correct. Short cues help the speaker present without reading a full script.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q39::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support and reasoning pair is strongest?
{
~A. Support: practice is good. Reasoning: it is good.#This repeats without explanation.
~B. Support: notes exist. Reasoning: notes exist.#This repeats and is vague.
=C. Support: a timer shows section length. Reasoning: the speaker can revise parts that run long.#Correct. This gives specific support and explains why it matters.
~D. Support: speeches happen. Reasoning: they happen.#This does not develop a point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Assessment_Q40::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which statement best summarizes Unit 2 mastery?
{
=A. A strong informative speech plan has focus, organization, support, paraphrase accuracy, transitions, and quality format.#Correct. This summarizes Unit 2 mastery.
~B. Support and transitions are not important.#They are essential to clear informative speaking.
~C. Paraphrase means changing the source meaning.#Paraphrase must keep meaning accurate.
~D. A speech plan only needs a topic.#A topic alone is incomplete.
}